# mimalloc, forked. Two blocks of rules, kept in that order:
#
#   1. upstream's own ignore conventions, in full and unweakened, plus the
#      re-inclusions that keep this fork's package content out of their reach;
#   2. this fork's build and tooling output.
#
# Nothing above the second block removes an upstream entry to make room for a
# fork-owned one. That rule is `AGENTS.md` §4 and it is the reason the
# re-inclusions are written as explicit negations rather than as a shorter
# upstream list.
#
# The one judgement call worth naming up front: **every upstream entry below was
# checked against this package's shipped file set, and none of them currently
# matches anything this package ships.** The re-inclusions in the middle are
# therefore forward-looking, not a repair of a present collision — and they are
# written now rather than at the moment someone needs them, because the moment
# someone adds a `docs/` directory is exactly the moment nobody reads a
# `.gitignore`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Upstream's own ignore conventions, kept verbatim.
#
# These are mimalloc's, for the v3.5.3 tree. Upstream's `.gitignore` is not one
# of the 52 files this package vendors — `UPSTREAM.md` imports the compiled and
# installed closure, not upstream's repository metadata — so without this file
# these conventions would simply be lost when the fork replaced upstream's. They
# are kept for that reason, not because this package produces any of them.
#
# A pattern with no slash matches at any depth, so each of these is a rule about
# every such name in the tree, not only about one at the root. That is why the
# per-entry notes below say what each one would catch and where.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Upstream's build directory. Matches any path component named exactly `build`,
# at any depth, file or directory.
#
# Safe here, and worth saying why rather than leaving it to be assumed:
# `build.zig`, `build.zig.zon`, `consumer/build.zig` and `consumer/build.zig.zon`
# are all package content and all four are shipped. `build` does not match
# `build.zig` -- a pattern matches a whole path component, not a prefix of one.
# A future file named exactly `build` would be caught, and that would be
# upstream's convention doing what it does everywhere else.
build

# Upstream's `out/` install directory. This fork's equivalent is `zig-out/`,
# which is named separately below; nothing this package ships lives under an
# `out/` directory, so this rule is inert today.
out/

# Upstream's generated documentation directory. This is the first of the two
# broad upstream entries, and it is re-included below.
docs/

# Upstream's compressed release artifacts. This is the second broad entry, and
# it is re-included below.
*.zip
*.tar
*.gz

# Editor and operating-system junk, both of which upstream carries and both of
# which name nothing this package ships.
.vscode
.DS_Store

# Upstream's Visual Studio project files under `ide/`. This package does not
# vendor `ide/` at all -- see `UPSTREAM.md`, "What was NOT taken" -- so the rule
# is inert, and it is kept because upstream's ignore convention is preserved
# rather than trimmed to what happens to match today.
ide/vs20??/*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-inclusions.
#
# These are the only lines standing between upstream's broad entries and this
# fork's own package content. They negate the rule; they do not delete it, so a
# file that *should* be ignored for an upstream reason can still be ignored by
# name further down.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `docs/` is un-ignored, because this package's documentation is content and a
# later wave must not lose it to upstream's convention.
#
# Today all eight documents sit at the repository root -- README.md,
# README.zig.md, NOTICE.md, UPSTREAM.md, UPSTREAM.sha256, FORK-LICENSE.md,
# CHANGELOG.md and FORK-NOTES.md -- and none is under a `docs/` directory, so
# this is a re-inclusion that currently matches nothing. It is written now for
# the case that does: a rendered manual, an example gallery or a generated
# reference under `docs/`. A directory has to be un-ignored *before* its
# contents can be, which is why the directory line and the contents line are
# both here.
!docs/
!docs/**

# `*.gz` is un-ignored, with the reason spelled out because it looks wrong at
# first glance.
#
# This package ships no compressed file: the release tarball is verified by its
# published sha256 and then unpacked into `src/`, `include/` and the three root
# records, and `UPSTREAM.sha256` is the record of those bytes rather than the
# archive. So un-ignoring `*.gz` cannot expose a vendored archive today. It
# matters in both directions later: a `.gz` that is *added* is package content
# and must be visible, and a `.gz` that a *build* produces is output.
#
# The two are separated without weakening the rule, because of how git applies
# this file: a directory that is itself excluded is never descended into, so a
# negation here cannot re-include anything inside one. That is why the build
# output block below ignores whole directories rather than patterns, and it is
# the property that makes this line safe to add.
!*.gz

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. This fork's own build and tooling output.
#
# These are directories rather than single files because a normal `zig build`
# creates them, and a normal `zig build` must not dirty the tree or leak into
# the package published from it. `build.zig.zon`'s `.paths` names exact files
# and never a directory, so an ignored path cannot become part of the package --
# the same reason that list has no `.` in it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.zig-cache/
zig-out/

# Zig 0.16 materialises a fetched dependency into a project-local directory
# beside the build root. This package declares no dependencies
# (`build.zig.zon` `.dependencies = .{}`), so this exists only so that a future
# one cannot commit a fetched tree.
zig-pkg/

# The consumer package builds inside this repository and has its own cache and
# output tree. It is ignored here as well as in consumer/.gitignore, so a build
# run from the repository root cannot commit it by accident.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/
consumer/zig-pkg/

# Nix and devenv working-tree artefacts.
#
# `nix build` writes `result` (and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild) into
# the directory that asked for it, and `devenv` writes its resolved inputs and
# its state beside the project. Neither is named in `build.zig.zon` `.paths`.
#
# `flake.lock` and `devenv.lock` are deliberately **not** here: they are real
# inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would be the wrong way to
# keep a build out of a commit.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
.direnv/